    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d162">

                <pb facs="0141" n="129" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg129"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">810.20 Defense/37<hi rendition="#superscript"
                        >9/17</hi>: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Haiti</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Sparks</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Port-au-Prince</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-08-24T22:00:00-05:00">August 24, 1940—10 p.m. [<hi
                                rendition="#italic">a.m.?</hi>]</date><lb/>[Received 12:11
                        p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>134. From Colonel Harris to Colonel Ridgway. Haitian Government authorizes army
                    plane to photograph airfields, ports, and harbors. May arrive any time. Will be
                    based at Port-au-Prince.<anchor n="78" xml:id="d162-f1"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sparks</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d162-f1" n="78" xml:id="d162-f1-content">
                <p>Despatches No. 971, August 26, 1940, No. 972, August 27, 1940, and No. 973,
                    August 27, 1940, report the successful conclusion of the army and navy staff
                    talks with the Government of Haiti (810.20 Defense/37<hi
                        rendition="#superscript">11/17</hi>, 37<hi rendition="#superscript"
                        >12/17</hi>. and 37<hi rendition="#superscript">13/17</hi>).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
